nquilla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg"><span class="enc-name">Magdalensberg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Magdalensberg is a mountain in Carinthia, Austria, whose summit and slopes hold the remains of a large Celtic and early Roman settlement.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magdeburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magdeburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Magdeburg is a city on the Elbe and the capital of Saxony-Anhalt, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg-borde"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magdeburg-borde.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magdeburg Börde</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Magdeburg Börde is a fertile lowland in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany, stretching west of Magdeburg and the Elbe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magellans-cross"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magellans-cross.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magellan's Cross</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Magellan's Cross is a Christian cross planted by the expedition of Ferdinand Magellan in Cebu, Philippines, in April 1521, at the baptism of Rajah Humabon and his people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta"><span class="enc-name">Maghan Kon Fatta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maghan Kon Fatta is the king of the Manding town of Niani remembered in the oral epic of Sundiata as the father of Sundiata Keita.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maghreb"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maghreb.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maghreb</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Maghreb is the region of north-west Africa comprising present-day Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia and Libya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magna Carta</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Magna Carta is the charter of liberties that rebellious barons forced King John of England to accept at Runnymede in June 1215.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-graecia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magna Graecia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Magna Graecia, Latin for Great Greece, was the name given to the coastal areas of southern Italy settled by Greek colonists from the 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnates"><span class="enc-name">Magnates</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnates were the great noble landowners of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, whose vast estates gave them enormous wealth and influence.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnentius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnentius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnentius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnentius was a Roman usurper who seized power in the West in January 350 after the murder of Emperor Constans, ruling Gaul, Britain, Spain, Italy and Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass"><span class="enc-name">Magnetic compass</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The magnetic compass is a navigational instrument that uses a magnetised needle to indicate direction.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnis"><span class="enc-name">Magnis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnis was a small walled Roman town at Kenchester, west of Hereford in England, on the road along the Welsh border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnitogorsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnitogorsk is an industrial city in the southern Urals of Russia, built from 1929 around a huge iron and steel works beside Magnitnaya, the 'magnetic mountain' of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Barefoot</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Barefoot, or Magnus III, was king of Norway from 1093 to 1103.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-decentius"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Decentius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Decentius was a relative, probably the brother, of the Roman usurper Magnentius, who made him Caesar in 350 and gave him command in Gaul.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-eriksson"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-eriksson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Eriksson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Eriksson (1316–1374) inherited the crown of Norway and was elected king of Sweden in 1319 as a three-year-old, uniting the two kingdoms under one king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Erlendsson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Erlendsson was an Earl of Orkney who shared rule of the islands with his cousin Håkon Paulsson.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Erlingsson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Erlingsson (1156–1184) was king of Norway from 1161, a son of the powerful nobleman Erling Skakke and a grandson of King Sigurd the Crusader through his mother.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-lagabotes-land-law"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Lagabøte's Land Law</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Lagabøte's Land Law (Landsloven) was the national law code issued for Norway by King Magnus VI in 1274, replacing the separate laws of the regional assemblies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Maximus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Maximus was a Roman general of Spanish origin who, as commander in Britain, was proclaimed emperor by his troops in 383.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-olafsson"><span class="enc-name">Magnus Olafsson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus Olafsson was the last king of Mann and the Isles of the Crovan dynasty, reigning from 1254 to 1265.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good"><span class="enc-name">Magnus the Good</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus the Good was king of Norway from 1035 and of Denmark from 1042 to 1047, the son of Olaf II, Norway's patron saint.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus VI of Norway</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus VI, called the Lawmender, was King of Norway from 1263 to 1280.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-duke-of-holstein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-duke-of-holstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magnus, Duke of Holstein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Magnus, Duke of Holstein, was a younger brother of Frederick II of Denmark, who bought him the bishoprics of Ösel-Wiek and Courland in 1559.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mago-agricultural-writer"><span class="enc-name">Mago (agricultural writer)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mago was a Carthaginian writer on agriculture who composed a large treatise on farming.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mago-i"><span class="enc-name">Mago I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mago I was a Carthaginian leader of the sixth century BCE, active around 550 to 530 BCE, and founder of the Magonid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magonid-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Magonid dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Magonid dynasty was a leading family of Carthage, founded by Mago I in the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maguire-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Maguire dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Maguires, Mág Uidhir, were the ruling family of Fermanagh in Ireland from the late 13th century to the early 17th, with their seat at Enniskillen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magway-region"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magway-region.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magway Region</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Magway Region is an administrative region of central Myanmar, in the dry zone along the Irrawaddy, with its capital at Magway.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/magyarisation"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/magyarisation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Magyarisation</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Magyarisation was the policy of spreading the Hungarian language and identity among the non-Hungarian peoples of the Kingdom of Hungary, pursued especially after the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/magyars"><span class="enc-name">Magyars</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Magyars are the Hungarian people, who settled the Carpathian Basin in the late ninth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-bandula"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-bandula.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maha Bandula</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Bandula was a Burmese general of the Konbaung dynasty who led campaigns in Manipur and Assam and on the frontier of British Bengal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-manikya"><span class="enc-name">Maha Manikya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Manikya was a ruler of Tripura in the early 15th century, usually seen as the first historically attested king of the Manikya dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maha Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Singh was chief of the Sukerchakia misl, one of the Sikh confederacies of the Punjab, from the early 1770s until his death in 1790.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-sura-singhanat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-sura-singhanat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maha Sura Singhanat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Sura Singhanat, born Bunma and known in the Thonburi period as Chao Phraya Surasi, was a Siamese general and the younger brother of Rama I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-ii"><span class="enc-name">Maha Thammaracha II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Thammaracha II was king of Sukhothai in the later fourteenth century, the son of Li Thai.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-iii"><span class="enc-name">Maha Thammaracha III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Thammaracha III, also known as Sai Lue Thai, was king of Sukhothai from about 1398 to 1419, as a vassal of Ayutthaya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-iv"><span class="enc-name">Maha Thammaracha IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Thammaracha IV was the last king of Sukhothai, ruling from 1419 to 1438 as a vassal of Ayutthaya after King Intharacha settled a succession dispute in his favour.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammarachathirat"><span class="enc-name">Maha Thammarachathirat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maha Thammarachathirat was king of Ayutthaya from 1569 to 1590.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maha Yazawin</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Maha Yazawin (Great Chronicle) is the first national chronicle of Burma, compiled by U Kala and completed around 1724 under the Restored Toungoo dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahabad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahabad is a city in West Azerbaijan province in north-western Iran, south of Lake Urmia, with a mainly Kurdish population.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabat-khanji-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabat-khanji-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahabat Khanji III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahabat Khanji III was the last ruling Nawab of Junagadh, from 1911 to 1948.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahabharata</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahabharata is one of the two great Sanskrit epics of ancient India, traditionally attributed to the sage Vyasa and composed over several centuries between roughly…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahadhammayaza-dipati"><span class="enc-name">Mahadhammayaza Dipati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahadhammayaza Dipati was the last king of the Toungoo dynasty of Burma, reigning from 1733 to 1752.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahadji Scindia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahadji Scindia was the Maratha ruler of Gwalior and the most powerful Indian chief of the late 18th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahajanga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahajanga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahajanga, formerly Majunga, is a port city on the north-west coast of Madagascar at the mouth of the Betsiboka River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahajirstvo"><span class="enc-name">Mahajirstvo</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahajirstvo is the name given to the mass emigration and deportation of Muslim peoples of the north-western Caucasus, including the Abkhaz and Circassians, to the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahakaccana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakaccana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahakaccana</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahakaccana was one of the leading disciples of the Buddha, praised in the Pali texts as foremost in explaining briefly stated teachings in detail.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakam-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahakam River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahakam River is the main river of East Kalimantan on Borneo, flowing about 900 kilometres from the interior highlands to the Makassar Strait.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahakuta-group-of-temples"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakuta-group-of-temples.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahakuta group of temples</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahakuta group of temples is a complex of Shaiva temples near Badami in northern Karnataka, India, built by the Chalukyas of Badami in the sixth and seventh…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahamati-prannath"><span class="enc-name">Mahamati Prannath</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahamati Prannath was a religious teacher from Jamnagar in Gujarat who led the Pranami sect, a Krishna-centred devotional movement founded by his guru Devchandra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahamatra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahamatra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahamatra</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahamatra was a title for senior officials in the Maurya Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahamuni-buddha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahamuni-buddha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahamuni Buddha</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahamuni Buddha is a seated bronze image of the Buddha, the most revered statue in Myanmar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahanadi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahanadi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahanadi is a major river of east-central India, about 850 kilometres long, rising in Chhattisgarh and flowing through Odisha to the Bay of Bengal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahanaga"><span class="enc-name">Mahanaga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahanaga was, according to the Mahavamsa, a brother of King Devanampiya Tissa of Anuradhapura who in the 3rd century BCE fled south after an attempt on his life and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahanaim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahanaim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahanaim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahanaim was a town in Gilead, east of the Jordan, mentioned in the Hebrew Bible.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahanandin"><span class="enc-name">Mahanandin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahanandin was, according to the Puranas, the last king of the Shishunaga dynasty of Magadha, reigning in the 4th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahapadma Nanda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahapadma Nanda was the founder of the Nanda dynasty of Magadha, who around 345 BCE overthrew the last Shishunaga king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahaparinirvana-sutra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mahaparinirvana-sutra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahaparinirvana Sutra</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mahaparinirvana Sutra, or Nirvana Sutra, is a Mahayana Buddhist scripture set during the last days of the Buddha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maharaja"><span class="enc-name">Maharaja</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Maharaja ('great king') is a Sanskrit royal title used across South Asia, originally for major sovereigns and later by many Hindu and Sikh rulers of princely states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maharaja-sayajirao-university-of-baroda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/maharaja-sayajirao-university-of-baroda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda is a public university in Vadodara, Gujarat, founded in 1949 and named after Sayajirao Gaekwad III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maharaja-sukarama"><span class="enc-name">Maharaja Sukarama</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maharaja Sukarama was a king of Negara Daha in southern Borneo known from the Hikayat Banjar chronicle.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja"><span class="enc-name">Maharajadhiraja</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Maharajadhiraja, meaning 'great king of kings', was an Indian imperial title.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maharana"><span class="enc-name">Maharana</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Maharana, 'great rana', was the royal title of the rulers of Mewar, whose capital was Chittor and from 1559 Udaipur, and of a few other Rajput states such as Porbandar.</span></a></li></ul>
